# EXPECTED-TO-FAIL — TODO.md §3, "The amber dot comes back after an app restart"
#
# docs/NATIVE-AGENTS.md §3.3 — the amber mark means "this needs you", and reading a thread is
#   what clears it
#
# `AgentMarkSeen` validates its cursor and records nothing: `seen` has a `client_id` column that
# nothing can fill, because `HelloClient` carries no per-install identity. The read cursor
# therefore lives only in app memory, and the app's own mitigation
# (`workspace/agent/requests.rs::resend_seen_cursors`) does not save it.
#
# The TODO entry names an *app* restart; the harness has no line that restarts Fleet, so this
# scenario exercises the same root cause through the reconnect the grammar does have. It is the
# same cursor and the same fix: the entry's own cost bullet is "`HelloClient.client_id` and a
# `seen_seq` on the window response **so a reconnecting client gets its own cursor back**".
#
# Fails today at the final `assert`, and only there: a thread read before the link dropped comes
# back `unread` with attention `unread` after the daemon restarts, and stays that way — measured
# stable at 2 s, 10 s and 25 s past the reconnect.
fixture: agents
await idle
key o
await screen == Workspace 20000

key ctrl-s a
await agents.threads[0].provider == claude 20000
click agents.composer
type first question
key enter

# Watched from start to finish, so the thread is read: no mark, no attention. Both edges of the
# turn are awaited in order, because awaiting `idle` alone holds before the turn has started.
await agents.threads[0].state == working 20000
await agents.threads[0].state == idle 40000
assert agents.threads[0].unread == false && agents.threads[0].state == idle
dump read

# The daemon goes away and comes back. Nothing the user read has changed.
daemon restart
await daemon.link == reconnected 60000
wait 5000
assert agents.threads[0].unread == false && agents.threads[0].state == idle
dump still-read
quit
